# Spring Boot I18N Engine

## Naming Conventions
- Name message-source component classes with the `*Messages` or `*LogMessages` suffix (e.g., `ApplicationMessages`, `UserLogMessages`).
- Use the `*LogMessages` suffix explicitly for log-event resolution components (e.g., `PaymentLogMessages`, `OrderLogMessages`).
- Use domain-specific component names (never `Messages`, `AppMessages`, or `LogMessages` without a domain prefix).

## Rules
- Set `spring.messages.basename=i18n/messages` in `application.yml`.
- Set `spring.messages.encoding=UTF-8` in `application.yml`.
- Place message bundles under `src/main/resources/i18n/`; name locale variants `messages.properties`, `messages_pt_BR.properties`, etc.
- Use UTF-8-safe content in all message bundle files.
- Use `# Section Name` comments inside `.properties` files to group keys by namespace.
- Keep key naming stable and domain-oriented.
- Use the `error.validation.*` namespace for Bean Validation constraint messages.
- Use the `error.*` namespace for domain errors; keep error text only in the bundle.
- Use the `api.*` namespace for API response messages.
- Use the `openapi.*` namespace for API documentation strings.
- Use the `log.*` namespace for log-event messages.
- Keep placeholders indexed and consistent across all locale variants.
- Keep identical key sets across all locale variants; add every new key to all locale files simultaneously.
- Use `Accept-Language` as the default locale negotiation strategy for API and web flows.
- Use `en` and `pt-BR` as baseline defaults unless the project explicitly defines a different supported locale set.
- Resolve all user-facing text in Java classes through message keys.
- Declare each `*LogMessages` component as a `public class` annotated with `@Component`.
- Place each `*LogMessages` component in an `i18n` sub-package of the module root or owning feature.
- Place a module-root `*LogMessages` component (e.g., `AppLogMessages`) in `<root-package>.i18n` when the component is consumed by cross-cutting shared classes such as `@RestControllerAdvice` or `@ControllerAdvice`.
- Use feature-scoped `*LogMessages` components (e.g., `HolidayLogMessages` in `holiday.i18n`) only when the component is consumed exclusively within that feature.
- Use dedicated `*LogMessages` components backed by `MessageSource` with `Locale.ENGLISH` for log events.
- Expose a `get(String key, Object... args)` method on each `*LogMessages` component that resolves messages with `Locale.ENGLISH`.
- Expose a `get(Locale locale, String key, Object... args)` overload on each `*LogMessages` component for caller-specified locale resolution.
- Keep the locale-aware overload signature fixed as `get(Locale locale, String key, Object... args)`.
- Implement `get(String key, Object... args)` by delegating to `get(Locale.ENGLISH, key, args)`.
- Keep `messages_pt_BR.properties` entries written in natural Portuguese with correct orthography and diacritics.
- Add `log.*` keys only when the same change wires those keys through a `*LogMessages` component used by application code.
- Add `openapi.*` keys only when the same change includes an OpenAPI component governed by a component-creation rule.
- Add `error.validation.*` and `error.*` keys only when the same change includes the controller or service component that produces those validation errors or domain failures.

## Safety Guards
- Never set `spring.messages.use-code-as-default-message=true`.
- Never remove keys that are still referenced.
- Never concatenate string fragments to construct a user-facing message.
- Never import a feature-scoped `*LogMessages` component from a class in the `shared` package; use the module-root `*LogMessages` instead.
